THE AI FUTURE IS FOR EVERYONE WHO ACCEPTS THE TERMS OF SERVICE

Centralised Power, Engagement Extraction, and the Sudden Democratic Awakening of Mark Zuckerberg

White Paper No. 62–META
Classification: Universally Empowering, Centrally Controlled
Prepared by: Office of Retrospective Principles, Institute for Monetised Human Potential
Date: 29 July 2026

⸻

Executive Summary

This paper examines Mark Zuckerberg’s proposal that artificial intelligence should be widely distributed because “centralized power stifles human potential.”

This is an intriguing position from the founder, chairman and chief executive of Meta, whose own annual report states that he controls a majority of the company’s voting power and can determine the outcome of shareholder decisions, elect directors, control management, approve major strategic investments and decide whether Meta is merged or sold. Meta consequently qualifies as a Nasdaq “controlled company,” exempting it from some ordinary independent-governance requirements. 

In other words, Zuckerberg’s theory appears to be:

Concentrated power is dangerous when somebody else might possess it.

Meta’s actual commercial record also complicates its new role as custodian of individual freedom. In 2025, advertising supplied approximately 97.6% of Meta’s total revenue: $196.175 billion out of $200.966 billion. Meta expressly tells investors that its revenue depends on user engagement, time spent, ad impressions, targeting and measurement. 

The company therefore does not merely empower individuals. It measures them, predicts them, ranks what they see, increases the quantity of monetisable interactions and converts those interactions into advertising revenue.

This is the business architecture criticised in The Social Dilemma. The film is a documentary-drama hybrid, not a court judgment, and its dramatic scenes establish nothing by themselves. Unfortunately for Meta, the central engagement-to-advertising mechanism described by the film is also described—without ominous music—inside Meta’s filings to the US Securities and Exchange Commission. 

Zuckerberg is unquestionably qualified to discuss how to build, scale, acquire and monetise digital platforms. Nothing in that achievement automatically qualifies him to determine what constitutes freedom, democratic balance, child wellbeing, public safety or a desirable future for humanity.

A fortune demonstrates that a commercial mechanism generated a fortune.

It is not a character reference.

