1. The Anti-Centralisation Sermon From the Controlling Shareholder

Zuckerberg begins from the proposition that centralised power suppresses human potential.

This argument is not inherently absurd. The speaker is.

Meta’s own 2025 annual report says Zuckerberg possesses voting control over the company and can determine shareholder outcomes, director elections, mergers, asset sales, management and major strategic investments. It further warns investors that his preferred decisions may differ from those supported by other shareholders. 

Meta’s Class B shares carry ten votes each, while ordinary Class A shares carry one. Because Meta is a controlled company, it is not required to maintain all the independent-board and committee arrangements ordinarily expected under Nasdaq governance rules. 

So before analysing the future constitutional structure of superintelligence, we can study the existing Zuckerberg model of distributed power:

Mark Zuckerberg:
    Majority voting control
Ordinary shareholders:
    The opportunity to express themselves before losing the vote

The man warning that a small number of institutions might exercise controlling influence over economics, science and politics already governs one of the world’s largest communications institutions through a capital structure designed to prevent anyone else from exercising equivalent control over him.

Apparently, absolute power becomes healthy when issued in Class B shares.

His essay asks society to reject the idea that one benevolent authority should decide what is best for everyone. Meta’s governance model replies:

Correct. There should be one authority, and it should be Mark.

Democracy, it seems, is an excellent operating system for civilisation but an unnecessary plug-in for Meta Platforms, Inc.

