2. Human Potential, Now Denominated in Advertising Impressions

Zuckerberg describes a future in which technology helps individuals pursue their aspirations.

Meta’s accounts describe those individuals somewhat differently.

They are daily active people, engagement levels, impressions, actions, targeting signals and average revenue per person. Meta reported that advertising produced $196.175 billion of its $200.966 billion in 2025 revenue. It attributed advertising growth partly to increased engagement, more impressions and improvements in targeting and measurement. 

Meta also warns investors that advertising revenue may decline if people spend less time on its products, if engagement falls, if fewer advertisements can be shown or if regulation restricts targeting and measurement. 

The commercial transformation is therefore:

Human attention
      ↓
Measurable engagement
      ↓
Advertisable impressions and actions
      ↓
Targeting and prediction
      ↓
Revenue

This is not a conspiracy theory. It is the declared business model.

Meta calls people a community when speaking to the public and a monetisation surface when speaking to investors.

That distinction matters because Zuckerberg now asks the public to accept Meta’s judgment about how superintelligence should be distributed. But Meta’s dominant institutional incentive has not historically been:

What arrangement maximises autonomous human flourishing?

It has been:

What product design sustains engagement, expands inventory and improves advertising performance?

Those objectives can occasionally coincide. They are not identical.

A recommendation system designed to maximise the probability that someone remains on a platform is not the same thing as a system designed to maximise that person’s welfare. The person may need sleep, exercise, privacy, quiet, reflection or an afternoon in which no multinational company computes an engagement probability for them.

The advertisement needs another impression.

Guess which participant has a quarterly target.

