3. The Social Dilemma Was the Film; the 10-K Is the Confession

The Social Dilemma presented social platforms as systems competing for attention through prediction, recommendation and engagement-driven design. It used interviews with technology insiders alongside a fictionalised family narrative, so it should not be treated as forensic evidence. 

Meta’s SEC filings are considerably less cinematic but more useful.

They state that revenue depends on engagement and time spent. They describe advertisements sold according to impressions and user actions. They explain that changes reducing engagement may damage financial results, and that Meta varies the prominence and frequency of advertisements as part of its monetisation strategy. 

The essential relationship can be written without a documentary control room:

\text{Revenue} \propto
\text{Users}
\times
\text{Time spent}
\times
\text{Monetisable impressions}
\times
\text{Price per impression}

The algorithm does not have to hate anyone.

It merely has to optimise the quantity that pays it.

That is the structural problem Zuckerberg’s essay avoids. When a company earns almost all its money by selling access to attention, “empowering people” is always competing against “retaining people.” An empowered user may close the application. A successfully retained user sees another advertisement.

Meta has introduced safety tools, parental controls and wellbeing features. Yet the European Commission announced preliminary findings in July 2026 that Facebook and Instagram’s design may breach the Digital Services Act through features including infinite scrolling, autoplay, push notifications and highly personalised recommendation systems. These remain preliminary regulatory findings, but they are remarkably specific criticisms of the same engagement architecture Meta tells investors is economically important. 

The Social Dilemma’s sinister fictional operators were unnecessary.

The spreadsheet already had the motive.

