5. A Product So Beneficial That Governments Are Removing the Children

Zuckerberg compares artificial intelligence with aviation, electricity and personal computing.

Governments are not currently requiring electricity providers to remove every person under sixteen from the grid.

They are doing something close to that with Facebook and Instagram.

Australia’s social-media age restrictions came into force on 10 December 2025. Platforms including Facebook, Instagram and Threads must take reasonable steps to prevent Australians under sixteen from creating or maintaining accounts. Australia’s eSafety Commissioner says the policy targets design features that encourage excessive time on screens and expose young people to potentially harmful content. 

On 21 July 2026, both chambers of the French Parliament approved legislation preventing children under fifteen from opening social-media accounts, with the restriction intended to begin for new accounts in September. 

Vietnam has proposed further restrictions on posting and interacting for users under sixteen, while other governments are considering related age limits. 

This does not mean every jurisdiction has banned Meta’s products for children. It means the direction of travel is no longer remotely subtle.

Societies increasingly regard these services not as neutral communications utilities but as products whose design may require statutory age barriers.

Books are not being removed from children.

Bicycles are not being removed from children.

Electric pumps are not being removed from children.

Facebook and Instagram are.

That is an inconvenient market review for a chief executive explaining that his previous technological revolution demonstrates why society should trust his judgment regarding the next one.

