6. The Courtroom Is Also a Form of User Feedback

Meta’s own first-quarter 2026 filing describes litigation in multiple countries alleging that Facebook and Instagram cause “social media addiction,” particularly among users under eighteen, with associated mental-health and other harms. It records claims brought by individuals, states, school districts, municipalities and tribal nations. 

The filing also reports that:

* A March 2026 bellwether jury awarded $6 million in compensatory and punitive damages, allocating 70% to Meta and 30% to YouTube. Meta says it intends to appeal.
* A New Mexico jury ordered Meta to pay a $375 million civil penalty.
* Lawyers representing more than 100,000 individual claimants have submitted mass-arbitration demands relating to alleged Instagram addiction and associated harms.
* The European Commission issued preliminary findings questioning whether Meta adequately prevents children under thirteen from accessing Facebook and Instagram. 

At the time of writing, Tennessee is also telling a jury that Meta disregarded internal warnings while retaining autoplay, notifications and infinite scrolling. Meta disputes that account and argues that the cited documents show it was examining problems so it could address them. 

That distinction matters. Allegations are not verdicts, preliminary findings are not final decisions, and verdicts can be appealed.

It is nevertheless difficult to look at this volume of litigation, regulation, arbitration and age-restriction legislation and conclude that Facebook’s impact has been one long demonstration of Zuckerberg’s superior judgment concerning human welfare.

Meta’s defence is often that these are complicated social problems and the company cannot bear sole responsibility.

That is entirely compatible with the criticism.

Zuckerberg’s essay does not merely claim Meta should be permitted to sell another product. It presents him as a reliable interpreter of history, democracy, safety and human flourishing.

A person cannot claim limited responsibility whenever the consequences are ugly, then claim civilisational authorship whenever the prospects sound magnificent.

Either the platform’s design meaningfully shapes people or it does not.

Meta’s advertising department appears to have settled that question years ago.

