8. The Superintelligent-Lawyer Thought Experiment Fails Basic Algebra

Zuckerberg asks us to imagine a world where one person has a superintelligent lawyer, then another where everybody has one. He concludes that universal access would make justice substantially fairer.

This ignores the difference between equal tools and equal power.

Let:

P_i = R_i \times L_i

where:

* P_i is effective legal power;
* R_i is the person’s underlying resources;
* L_i is the leverage supplied by artificial intelligence.

Suppose a corporation and an individual receive exactly the same AI multiplier L:

\frac{P_{\text{corporation}}}{P_{\text{individual}}}
=
\frac{R_{\text{corporation}}L}
{R_{\text{individual}}L}
=
\frac{R_{\text{corporation}}}
{R_{\text{individual}}}

The inequality remains unchanged.

In reality, L would not be equal either. The corporation would have proprietary data, internal documents, specialist models, large compute budgets, professional legal teams, procedural experience, institutional relationships and the ability to sustain litigation for years.

The individual would have the free tier and a message saying:

You have 14 advanced legal queries remaining this week.

Giving everyone access to a calculator does not equalise their bank balances. Giving everyone an AI lawyer does not equalise discovery budgets, court fees, time, evidence, representation, bargaining power or the consequences of losing.

The thought experiment therefore demonstrates almost nothing about distributive justice.

It shows that Zuckerberg has multiplied both sides of an inequality by the same number and announced that the ratio disappeared.

