9. Open to Everyone, Through Meta’s Meter

Zuckerberg invokes open-source software as evidence that broad access creates safety and resilience.

Meta has certainly contributed important models and research to the wider ecosystem. But its repeated description of Llama as “open source” has been disputed by the Open Source Initiative, which says the Llama 3.x licensing terms do not satisfy the Open Source Definition. 

Meta’s current flagship model family also illustrates the difference between access and control. Muse Spark initially powered Meta’s own products and was offered in private API preview to selected partners. Muse Spark 1.1 subsequently entered a developer preview through Meta’s model API with tiered usage pricing. 

That is a perfectly ordinary product strategy.

It is not decentralised ownership.

The user can call the model. Meta controls the model.

The developer can purchase tokens. Meta controls the meter.

The public can use the assistant. Meta controls deployment, policies, ranking, availability, pricing and termination.

Zuckerberg’s proposal distributes endpoints, not governing power.

It gives everyone a road while Meta retains the land, tollbooth, traffic rules, surveillance cameras and right to close the motorway.

Calling that “balance of power” requires an unusually entrepreneurial definition of balance.

