10. The Myanmar Exception to the Inevitable Arc of Progress

Zuckerberg’s historical argument says transformative technologies repeatedly leave humanity with greater prosperity, health and freedom.

That is not a law. It is a selected montage.

Meta’s own commissioned human-rights assessment concerning Myanmar concluded that, before corrective efforts in 2018, Facebook had not done enough to prevent its platform from being used to “foment division and incite offline violence.” Meta publicly accepted that conclusion and said it needed to do more. 

Amnesty International later reached the more severe conclusion that Facebook’s algorithmic amplification and engagement-based business model substantially contributed to harms suffered by the Rohingya. 

The relevant point does not require attributing an entire national atrocity to one company.

It is that mass distribution of a powerful information system does not automatically produce liberty, knowledge or democratic balance. The outcome depends on incentives, moderation, local conditions, governance, language coverage, recommendation systems and the speed with which the operator reacts to foreseeable harm.

In other words, the technology did not become benevolent merely because many people received access.

That is the precise assumption Zuckerberg repeats in his superintelligence essay:

Put the capability in everyone’s hands, and their competing interests will naturally balance one another.

Myanmar showed that mass participation can also produce mass amplification, especially when the infrastructure rewards whatever keeps people reacting.

History does not bend toward freedom because a founder drew an upward arrow in an investor presentation.

Someone still has to engineer the system so it does not reward the fire.

