11. Decentralisation, Sole-Occupied by Meta

The timing of Zuckerberg’s essay deserves preservation in a museum.

On 28 July 2026—the same day he warned that superintelligence concentrated within a handful of institutions would exert unacceptable power—Meta announced a data-centre venture with BlackRock.

The El Paso campus is planned to provide one gigawatt of compute capacity, cost approximately $14 billion, and begin bringing capacity online in 2028. BlackRock-managed funds will own 80% of the venture, Meta 20%, and Meta will be the initial sole occupant and lease the entire campus. 

The public philosophy:

Superintelligence must not be controlled by a few enormous institutions.

The physical implementation:

Institution 1: Meta
Institution 2: BlackRock
Compute: 1 gigawatt
Initial occupants: Meta
Public voting rights over its operation: 0

Zuckerberg even described building this infrastructure as necessary to distribute the benefits of superintelligence to everyone. 

That sentence reveals the actual architecture.

Meta does not propose decentralised production.

It proposes:

\text{centralised ownership and computation}
\rightarrow
\text{distributed consumer access}

That is not a balance of power. It is a utility model.

Everyone may receive water.

Meta owns the reservoir, pipes, meter, treatment standard, account system and valve.

The essay turns that ordinary corporate arrangement into a theory of democracy by confusing the number of customers with the distribution of control.

McDonald’s serves millions of people.

That does not make its fryer democratically owned.

