12. Money Is Not a Moral Peer-Review Process

Zuckerberg is enormously wealthy because Facebook became an enormously successful business.

That establishes several things.

It establishes that he can scale infrastructure.

It establishes that he can recruit capital and talent.

It establishes that Meta can acquire competitors, operate massive platforms and monetise human attention at extraordinary scale.

It does not establish that he is kind.

It does not establish that his incentives match the public’s.

It does not establish that Facebook improved society more than it damaged it.

It does not establish that he should determine the governance model of superintelligence.

The relationship is:

\text{Commercial success} \neq \text{moral authority}

\text{Net worth} \neq \text{wisdom}

\text{Market dominance} \neq \text{democratic legitimacy}

A person may be extremely good at constructing a tollbooth without being the best person to write a philosophy of public roads.

Indeed, getting rich from a system can make someone a particularly poor neutral judge of whether that system served everyone else.

Meta generated more than $83 billion in operating income during 2025. That number proves the machine worked for Meta. 

It does not calculate the social result.

There is no accounting line beneath operating income marked:

Less:
Children compulsively scrolling
Political division
Loss of privacy
Fraud exposure
Mental-health effects
Social trust consumed
Human attention permanently unavailable for other purposes

Those costs appear elsewhere: in families, schools, hospitals, elections, regulators and courtrooms.

Then the founder publishes an essay explaining that the machine’s owner has studied the arc of history and concluded the next machine should also be everywhere.

What a fortunate result for the machine’s owner.

