13. The Actual Meta Philosophy

Stripped of historical costume, Zuckerberg’s proposal is straightforward:

Meta should be allowed to build extremely powerful AI, operate the infrastructure centrally, make the interface widely available and distinguish itself from rivals by presenting broad access as democratic decentralisation.

That is a business position.

It may even produce useful products.

But it is not the transfer of power Zuckerberg claims.

Users would not own the model.

Users would not govern the model.

Users would not vote on its objectives, training, policies, recommendation systems, monetisation, data retention or acceptable uses.

Users would receive permission to interact with it.

Meta would retain the meaningful powers:

Ownership
Infrastructure
Deployment
Terms
Pricing
Data
Ranking
Policy
Access
Revocation

The public receives the chat box.

This is the same conceptual trick Facebook has performed for years: describe a centrally designed and privately governed platform as though the fact that billions of people use it makes the institution itself democratic.

Participation is not governance.

Consumption is not ownership.

Access is not sovereignty.

Being permitted to speak inside someone else’s machine does not mean you control the machine.

