14. Final Determination

Mark Zuckerberg’s essay presents him as an opponent of concentrated technological power.

Meta’s corporate structure concentrates decisive power in Mark Zuckerberg.

The essay presents Meta as an institution committed to individual choice.

Meta has paid or agreed to pay billions in privacy penalties and settlements arising from disputes over whether people received meaningful control over their information.

The essay presents widespread access as a natural mechanism for safety.

Meta’s own history shows that widespread access combined with engagement-driven amplification can contribute to severe social harms.

The essay celebrates independent invention.

Several major pillars of Meta’s empire were acquired, while Facebook’s own origin generated a substantial legal dispute and settlement.

The essay claims that universal AI access will balance unequal institutions.

Basic algebra shows that applying the same leverage to unequal resources preserves the inequality.

The essay warns against superintelligence controlled by a handful of organisations.

On the same day, Meta and BlackRock announced a $14 billion, one-gigawatt compute campus initially occupied entirely by Meta.

This is not hypocrisy hidden several interpretive layers beneath the text.

It is standing in the lobby wearing a Meta Quest headset.

The central conclusion is therefore:

Mark Zuckerberg is not proposing the decentralisation of artificial intelligence. He is proposing the mass distribution of access to artificial intelligence whose infrastructure, governance and commercial relationship remain centrally controlled by Meta.

The distinction matters.

The AI future may indeed be for everyone.

That does not mean everyone owns it.

It means everyone becomes a user.

And Mark Zuckerberg has spent twenty years demonstrating that having billions of users is not remotely the same thing as distributing power among them.

